-- Q4 [Lineweaver-Burk (Double-Reciprocal) Plots] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Lineweaver-Burk (Double-Reciprocal) Plots', 'A researcher linearizes initial-velocity data for a purified enzyme using a Lineweaver-Burk (double-reciprocal) plot of \(1/v_0\) versus \(1/[S]\). The fitted line has a y-intercept of \(1/V_{max} = 0.01\ \text{min}\cdot\mu\text{M}^{-1}\). Based on this intercept, the enzyme''s \(V_{max}\) is most likely closest to which value?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "0.01 \\(\\mu\\text{M/min}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "1 \\(\\mu\\text{M/min}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "10 \\(\\mu\\text{M/min}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "100 \\(\\mu\\text{M/min}\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because on a Lineweaver-Burk plot the y-intercept equals \(1/V_{max}\), so \(V_{max}\) is recovered by taking the reciprocal of that intercept, not by reading the intercept value directly. The textbook states that for plots of \(1/v\) vs \(1/S\) the lines are straight with ''slope m = KM/VM, and y-intercept b = 1/VM'' (line 27476), and that these plots ''are used to estimate VM from the 1/v intercept (1/VM) and KM from the 1/S axis (-1/KM)'' (line 26959). Given a y-intercept of \(1/V_{max} = 0.01\ \text{min}\cdot\mu\text{M}^{-1}\), inverting gives \(V_{max} = 1/0.01 = 100\ \mu\text{M/min}\); the units also invert from min/µM to µM/min, confirming the magnitude. (Choice A) is incorrect: 0.01 µM/min simply reports the y-intercept value itself, but the intercept is \(1/V_{max}\), not \(V_{max}\), so this is the ''read the intercept directly'' trap that omits the required reciprocal step (line 27476). (Choice B) is incorrect: 1 µM/min would result from mis-inverting only part of the value or treating the intercept as already two orders of magnitude larger; the correct reciprocal of 0.01 is 100, not 1. (Choice C) is incorrect: 10 µM/min reflects an order-of-magnitude error in the reciprocal, as though \(1/0.01\) were taken as 10 rather than 100. (Choice D) is correct: \(V_{max} = 1/(\text{y-intercept}) = 1/0.01 = 100\ \mu\text{M/min}\), consistent with the cited identity y-intercept \(= 1/V_M\) (lines 27476, 26959). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Lineweaver-Burk intercept relationship and perform a reciprocal computation rather than read a value off the axis.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
