-- Q6 [Lineweaver-Burk (Double-Reciprocal) Plots] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Lineweaver-Burk (Double-Reciprocal) Plots', 'A pharmacology lab assays two purified enzymes that act on the same substrate and plots the initial-rate data for each as a Lineweaver-Burk (double-reciprocal) line. Enzyme 1''s line crosses the y-axis at \(0.005\ \text{min}\cdot\mu M^{-1}\), whereas Enzyme 2''s line crosses the y-axis at \(0.020\ \text{min}\cdot\mu M^{-1}\). Assuming both assays were run identically, which enzyme most likely has the higher \(V_{max}\)?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Enzyme 2, because its larger y-intercept corresponds to a larger \\(V_{max}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Enzyme 1, because its smaller y-intercept corresponds to a larger \\(V_{max}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Neither, because \\(V_{max}\\) cannot be compared from the y-intercepts alone"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Enzyme 1, because the y-intercept reports \\(K_m\\) and a smaller \\(K_m\\) means a larger \\(V_{max}\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because on a Lineweaver-Burk plot the y-intercept equals \(1/V_{max}\), so a smaller y-intercept must correspond to a larger \(V_{max}\). The textbook states that for plots of \(1/v\) vs \(1/S\), the ''y-intercept b = 1/VM'' and that these plots ''are used to estimate VM from the 1/v intercept (1/VM)'' (lines 27476, 26959). Inverting each intercept gives Enzyme 1 a \(V_{max}\) of \(1/0.005 = 200\ \mu M\cdot\text{min}^{-1}\) versus Enzyme 2''s \(1/0.020 = 50\ \mu M\cdot\text{min}^{-1}\); because the reciprocal relationship inverts the ordering, the enzyme with the smaller intercept (Enzyme 1) has the larger \(V_{max}\). (Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the reciprocal relationship — a larger y-intercept is \(1/V_{max}\) of a larger denominator''s inverse, i.e., it corresponds to a SMALLER \(V_{max}\), not a larger one, so Enzyme 2 actually has the lower \(V_{max}\) (line 27476: ''y-intercept b = 1/VM''). (Choice B) is correct: a smaller y-intercept equals a smaller \(1/V_{max}\), which inverts to a larger \(V_{max}\), so Enzyme 1 wins (lines 26959, 27476). (Choice C) is incorrect: \(V_{max}\) is directly recoverable from the y-intercept as its reciprocal, so the two enzymes can be compared from the intercepts alone — the text explicitly says VM is estimated ''from the 1/v intercept (1/VM)'' (line 26959). (Choice D) is incorrect: the y-intercept reports \(1/V_{max}\), not \(K_m\); \(K_m\) is read from the x-intercept (\(-1/K_m\)), so attributing the intercept to \(K_m\) misidentifies the axis even though it lands on the right enzyme by coincidence (line 26959: ''KM from the 1/S axis (-1/KM)''; line 27476: ''y-intercept b = 1/VM''). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the reciprocal relationship between a Lineweaver-Burk y-intercept and \(V_{max}\) to invert and compare two enzymes'' maximal velocities rather than merely recall a definition.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
