-- Q7 [Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)', 'A pharmacology student is comparing a classic monomeric Michaelis-Menten enzyme, which yields a hyperbolic rate curve, with a cooperative (allosteric) enzyme that yields a sigmoidal rate curve. Which structural feature most likely distinguishes the cooperative enzyme from the simple monomeric one?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It has only a single active site shared across the protein"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It has no active site and instead binds substrate nonspecifically"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It has multiple subunits, each containing its own active site"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It is always monomeric, with no quaternary structure"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because cooperative (allosteric) enzymes that produce sigmoidal kinetics are multimeric proteins built from multiple subunits, with each subunit contributing its own active site for the substrate. The textbook states that the multiple binding sites for a main ligand ''can be found on a multimeric protein with identical or similar subunits'' and that the ligand ''binds to the same active site in each monomer,'' i.e., the ''allosteric ligand binds at the active sites of the monomeric subunits of the protein'' (lines 20510-20520). It further describes this homotropic cooperativity in terms of a ''multisubunit protein'' whose substrate-binding influences ''the remaining unoccupied substrate binding sites'' (lines 21228-21230), which is precisely what gives sigmoidal kinetics rather than the hyperbolic curve of a single-site monomeric enzyme. (Choice A) is wrong: a single shared active site describes the simple monomeric Michaelis-Menten enzyme, not the cooperative enzyme; cooperativity requires multiple sites distributed across subunits so that occupancy at one site can affect the others (lines 20510-20513). (Choice B) is wrong: by definition an enzyme has an active site, ''the part of an enzyme that directly binds to a substrate and carries out a reaction'' (lines 5268-5269), so ''no active site'' cannot describe any functional enzyme. (Choice C) is correct: cooperative allosteric enzymes are multimeric, with substrate binding at the active site of each of the several subunits, as the text describes for multisubunit allosteric proteins and for O2 binding ''at the active sites of the monomeric subunits'' (lines 20510-20520, 21228-21230). (Choice D) is wrong: classic sigmoidal cooperativity arises from a ''multimeric protein with identical or similar subunits'' (line 20512) and is contrasted with monomeric myoglobin, so a cooperative enzyme is not ''always monomeric'' and does possess quaternary structure. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the defining structural difference between a simple monomeric Michaelis-Menten enzyme and a multisubunit cooperative (allosteric) enzyme.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
