-- Q8 [Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)', 'A biochemist studies a multisubunit, cooperative enzyme in which binding of substrate to one subunit raises the affinity of the remaining subunits for substrate. When the initial velocity is measured across a wide range of substrate concentrations and plotted against \([S]\), which shape best describes the resulting velocity-versus-\([S]\) curve?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A rectangular hyperbola that rises steeply at low \\([S]\\) and then levels off toward \\(V_{max}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A straight line passing through the origin across the entire range of \\([S]\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An upward-opening parabola that keeps curving upward without ever plateauing"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A sigmoidal (S-shaped) curve"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because a cooperative (allosteric) enzyme, in which substrate binding to one subunit increases the affinity of the remaining subunits, produces an S-shaped kinetic curve rather than the simple hyperbola of a non-cooperative enzyme. LibreTexts states that ''In Homotropic or Type I allosterism, binding or kinetic curves show sigmoidal fractional saturation (or kinetic curves) with increasing substrate concentrations'' (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 21234-21235), and it contrasts this with the non-cooperative case, noting that plots of fractional saturation versus ligand are ''hyperbolic for myoglobin (Mb), but sigmoidal for Hb, suggesting cooperative binding of oxygen to Hb (binding of the first oxygen facilitates binding of the second, etc)'' (line 20391). The text reinforces this distinction with phosphofructokinase, where the allosteric isoform is cooperative while ''Pfk B shows hyperbolic kinetics and no allosterism, with a Hill coefficient close to 1'' (line 21266). (Choice A) is incorrect: a rectangular hyperbola that rises steeply and then levels off is the curve of a simple, non-cooperative Michaelis-Menten enzyme, where ''a plot of v vs. S is hyperbolic'' (line 26935) and ''It is a hyperbola just like the graph for the binding of a ligand to a macromolecule with a given dissociation constant, KD'' (lines 26920-26921); the hyperbolic, non-allosteric case is exemplified by Pfk B (line 21266), so this shape describes the absence of cooperativity, not its presence. (Choice B) is incorrect: velocity is only ''a linear function of S when S<<Ks'' (line 26942), so a straight line through the origin is just the low-\([S]\) approximation, not the full-range curve, which saturates at high \([S]\) rather than rising linearly forever. (Choice C) is incorrect: a parabola keeps curving upward without bound, whereas an enzyme''s velocity saturates and approaches \(V_{max}\) at high \([S]\) (''v = Vmax (or VM) when S is much greater than Ks'', line 26943), so no enzyme kinetic curve is a parabola. (Choice D) is correct: because cooperative substrate binding makes the early subunits hard to fill and later subunits easy to fill, the velocity-versus-\([S]\) curve is sigmoidal (S-shaped), exactly as the text describes for homotropic Type I allosterism and for cooperative Hb (lines 21234-21235, 20391). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that a cooperative enzyme produces a sigmoidal velocity-versus-\([S]\) curve, in contrast to the rectangular hyperbola of a simple Michaelis-Menten enzyme.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 45)
  RETURNING id
)
