-- Q9 [Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)', 'A biochemist is studying a cooperative, multisubunit enzyme whose subunits interconvert between a tense (T) state and a relaxed (R) state. The two conformations differ in how tightly they bind the enzyme''s substrate, which gives rise to the enzyme''s sigmoidal kinetics. Of these two conformational states, which one most likely has the HIGHER affinity for the substrate?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The R (relaxed) state"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The T (tense) state"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Both states bind substrate with equal affinity"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Neither state is able to bind substrate"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because in cooperative, multisubunit proteins the relaxed (R) state is the high-affinity conformation, so it binds substrate more tightly than the tense (T) state. LibreTexts describes the Monod-Wyman-Changeux model, in which "proteins exist in multiple preexisting conformations (e.g., taut and relaxed forms), with ligands preferentially binding to the high-affinity state" (line 23894-23895), and it labels these conformations the "T [tense] and R [relaxed] states" (line 21459-21460). The text further notes that ligand "binds preferentially to the R form" and that, without ligand, "the T state (T0) is greatly favored over the unliganded R state (R0)," while "in the presence of increasing oxygen, the R state is favored" (lines 20733, 20663-20664) — the hallmark of the R state''s higher affinity. (Choice A) is correct: the R (relaxed) state is the high-affinity conformation, so it binds substrate more tightly than the T state. (Choice B) is incorrect: the T (tense) state is the LOW-affinity conformation, the form that predominates in the absence of ligand because it binds substrate weakly, not strongly. (Choice C) is incorrect: the two states do not have equal affinity; their differing affinities (R high, T low) are precisely what produces the sigmoidal binding curve seen in "Homotropic or Type I allosterism," where "binding or kinetic curves show sigmoidal fractional saturation... with increasing substrate concentrations" (lines 21234-21235). (Choice D) is incorrect: both states can bind substrate, just with different affinities — the T state binds with low affinity rather than not binding at all (an idealized non-binding T state arises only "in the limiting case, when oxygen didn''t bind to the T form," line 20733-20734), so it is wrong to say neither state binds substrate. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the relaxed (R) state is the high-affinity conformation and the tense (T) state is the low-affinity conformation of a cooperative protein.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 55)
  RETURNING id
)
