-- Q10 [Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)', 'A researcher studies a four-subunit enzyme that shows positive cooperativity for its substrate. The researcher confirms that, before any substrate is present, the subunits sit in the low-affinity, tense (T) conformation, and that substrate binding to the first subunit is followed by an increase in the substrate affinity of the still-empty subunits. Which change in the remaining unoccupied subunits best accounts for this increase in affinity after the first substrate binds?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "They shift toward the low-affinity, tense (T) conformation"}, {"label": "B", "text": "They shift toward the high-affinity, relaxed (R) conformation"}, {"label": "C", "text": "They remain in their original conformation, unaffected by the first binding event"}, {"label": "D", "text": "They are proteolytically cleaved from the rest of the enzyme"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because, in a positively cooperative multisubunit protein, binding of the first ligand drives the still-empty subunits from the low-affinity tense (T) state toward the high-affinity relaxed (R) state, which is exactly the rise in affinity the stem describes. LibreTexts maps these states by affinity, naming the structural transitions "between the T (tense) and R (relaxed) states" and noting that "the binding of one oxygen molecule increases the affinity of the remaining sites (homotropic allosterism)" (lines 20980-20982). It states for the cooperative case that "the first one binds with some low affinity, which, through associated conformational changes, alters the remaining three sites to very high-affinity sites" (lines 20612-20613), and that in the KNF picture "ligand binding can induce conformational changes in adjacent subunits, promoting the cooperative binding of ligands" so that the protein is altered "to produce tighter binding" (lines 23640-23644); more generally, "oxygen binding to one of the subunits of hemoglobin... prompts cooperative binding to other subunits" (line 31128). (Choice A) is incorrect: the T conformation is the low-affinity state ("T (tense/deoxy)" versus "R (relaxed/oxy)", line 20031), so shifting the empty subunits toward T would lower their affinity and produce negative, not positive, cooperativity, the opposite of what is observed. (Choice B) is correct: the remaining subunits shift from the low-affinity T state toward the high-affinity R state, which raises their affinity for the next substrate, the defining feature of positive cooperativity (lines 20612-20613, 20980-20982, 23640-23644). (Choice C) is incorrect: leaving the empty subunits unchanged would mean the first binding event has no influence on the others, giving the non-cooperative, hyperbolic behavior of an enzyme whose sites are independent, whereas the stem specifies that the empty subunits gain affinity (lines 20980-20981). (Choice D) is incorrect: proteolytic cleavage is the irreversible covalent activation step that converts a zymogen, an "inactive precursor of an enzyme," into an active enzyme (lines 31298-31299); it is not the reversible T-to-R conformational change that underlies cooperative substrate binding, and nothing about cooperativity removes a subunit from the complex. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the T-to-R, low-to-high-affinity model of positive cooperativity to infer how the first binding event changes the conformation and affinity of the still-empty subunits.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
