-- Q11 [Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)] medium skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)', 'A biochemist characterizes a newly purified multisubunit enzyme and fits its substrate-binding data to the Hill equation, obtaining a best-fit Hill coefficient of approximately 2.8. Assuming this value is reliable, it most strongly indicates that the enzyme exhibits:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "negative cooperativity among its binding sites"}, {"label": "B", "text": "no cooperativity among its binding sites"}, {"label": "C", "text": "positive cooperativity among its binding sites"}, {"label": "D", "text": "competitive inhibition at its active site"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because a Hill coefficient greater than 1 reflects positive cooperativity, in which the binding of one ligand increases the affinity of the remaining sites and produces a sigmoidal binding curve. The reference text fits hemoglobin''s cooperative, sigmoidal oxygen binding with a Hill coefficient of n=2.8 ("using a Hill coefficient of n=2.8"; "If n is set to 2.8, the theoretical curve of Y vs L gives the best...fit"), and describes an allosterically activated PFK A with a Hill coefficient of 3.3, both Hill values well above 1. By contrast, the same text states the Hill equation "becomes: Y = L/[KD + L] when n =1 (as in the case with myoglobin)," i.e., a value of 1 corresponds to non-cooperative, hyperbolic binding, so a value near 2.8 cannot indicate the absence of cooperativity. (Choice A) is incorrect: the KNF model distinguishes positive cooperativity from negative cooperativity by the pattern of ligand affinities, not by a Hill coefficient above 1; negative cooperativity (decreasing affinity at successive sites) is associated with a Hill coefficient below 1, not 2.8. (Choice B) is incorrect: no cooperativity corresponds to a Hill coefficient of 1, as with myoglobin ("Y = L/[KD + L] when n =1") and the non-allosteric PFK B with a "Hill coefficient close to 1," whereas a value of 2.8 lies clearly above 1. (Choice C) is correct: a Hill coefficient greater than 1, such as 2.8 for hemoglobin or 3.3 for allosterically activated PFK A, signals positive cooperativity and sigmoidal binding. (Choice D) is incorrect: competitive inhibition is a separate phenomenon in which "substrate (S) and inhibitor (I) both bind to the same site on the enzyme" and is assessed from inhibition kinetics, not from the Hill coefficient, which reports on cooperativity of ligand binding. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that a Hill coefficient greater than 1 indicates positive cooperativity.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
