-- Q13 [Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)', 'A researcher fits the ligand-binding data for a newly purified enzyme to the Hill equation and finds that the best-fit Hill coefficient is equal to 1. Based on this value, the binding behavior of the enzyme is most likely characterized by which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "No cooperativity, producing a hyperbolic binding curve"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Positive cooperativity, producing a sigmoidal binding curve"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Negative cooperativity, producing a flattened binding curve"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Maximal cooperativity, producing a step-like binding curve"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because a Hill coefficient equal to 1 reflects non-cooperative, simple binding. The text states that ''the Hill equation becomes: Y = L/[KD + L] when n =1 (as in the case with myoglobin, and in any equilibrium expression of the form M+L↔ML,'' and that for n = 1, ''plots of ML vs L or Y vs L give hyperbolas'' (line 20615-20616). Likewise, the text describes an enzyme that ''shows hyperbolic kinetics and no allosterism, with a Hill coefficient close to 1'' (line 21266-21267), directly linking n ≈ 1 to the absence of cooperativity. (Choice A) is correct: a Hill coefficient of 1 indicates no cooperativity and yields a hyperbolic (non-sigmoidal) binding curve, exactly as described for myoglobin and for the non-allosteric enzyme in the text (lines 20615-20616, 21266-21267). (Choice B) is wrong: positive cooperativity produces a sigmoidal curve and requires a Hill coefficient greater than 1; the text fits hemoglobin''s sigmoidal, cooperative binding ''using a Hill coefficient of n=2.8'' (line 20770), a value above 1, not equal to it. (Choice C) is wrong: negative cooperativity (where later ligands bind with lower affinity, line 20862) corresponds to a Hill coefficient less than 1, not equal to 1, so n = 1 does not indicate negative cooperativity. (Choice D) is wrong: maximal cooperativity describes the extreme, ''infinitely cooperative binding of oxygen'' limit in which all sites fill essentially at once (line 20614), corresponding to a very high Hill coefficient, not a value of 1. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that a Hill coefficient equal to 1 signifies non-cooperative, hyperbolic binding rather than any form of cooperativity.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 45)
  RETURNING id
)
