-- Q14 [Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)', 'A biochemist characterizing a newly purified oligomeric enzyme finds that a plot of initial velocity versus substrate concentration is sigmoidal rather than hyperbolic, and a Hill-equation fit of the data yields a Hill coefficient of 2.8. Which interpretation of these two observations is best supported?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The enzyme has a single substrate-binding active site whose affinity is fixed."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The enzyme binds substrate with positive cooperativity across multiple binding sites."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The enzyme binds substrate with negative cooperativity, so each binding event lowers the affinity of the remaining sites."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The enzyme follows non-cooperative Michaelis-Menten kinetics with no interaction between sites."}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because a sigmoidal velocity curve together with a Hill coefficient well above 1 are the textbook signatures of positive cooperativity among multiple binding sites. LibreTexts states that "sigmoidal curves imply cooperative binding" and that as one ligand binds, "the next oxygen seems to bind with higher affinity (lower KD)" (lines 20533-20534), which is the definition of positive cooperativity. The same source notes that such cooperativity requires "multiple ligand binding sites on the same protein" found on a "multimeric protein with identical or similar subunits" (lines 20510-20512), and it directly ties an elevated Hill coefficient to allosteric, cooperative kinetics in an oligomeric enzyme, reporting a "Hill coefficient … 3.3 for Pfk A" that promotes "formation of the enzyme tetramer" (lines 21265-21266). A measured value of 2.8 (greater than 1) therefore points to positive cooperativity across several subunit active sites. (Choice A) is incorrect: a single fixed-affinity active site cannot produce a sigmoidal curve, because cooperativity requires "multiple ligand binding sites" on a "multimeric protein" (lines 20510-20512); the enzyme is explicitly oligomeric and behaves cooperatively, so the single-site reading contradicts the data. (Choice B) is correct: a sigmoidal v-versus-[S] curve and a Hill coefficient of 2.8 (> 1) indicate cooperative binding across multiple sites in which early binding raises the affinity of the remaining sites "with higher affinity (lower KD)" (lines 20533-20534). (Choice C) is incorrect: negative cooperativity would mean each binding event lowers the affinity of the remaining sites (the KNF figure contrasts "positive cooperativity" from "negative cooperativity," lines 20854-20862) and does not generate the rising-affinity, sigmoidal, Hill-coefficient-greater-than-1 pattern observed here. (Choice D) is incorrect: non-cooperative Michaelis-Menten behavior corresponds to a Hill coefficient of 1 and a hyperbolic curve—"the Hill equation becomes: Y = L/[KD + L] when n =1" giving hyperbolas (line 20615), and the source describes a comparable enzyme that "shows hyperbolic kinetics and no allosterism, with a Hill coefficient close to 1" (lines 21266-21267); a value of 2.8 with a sigmoidal curve rules this out. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between curve shape and Hill coefficient—integrating the sigmoidal shape and the value 2.8 (> 1) and rejecting the single-site trap—to infer positive cooperativity across multiple sites rather than non-cooperative or negatively cooperative binding.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
