-- Q15 [Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)', 'A four-subunit enzyme that binds its substrate with positive cooperativity is studied at a substrate concentration high enough that several subunits are occupied. The investigators then dilute the sample so that one bound substrate molecule dissociates from a subunit that had been holding it. Assuming the cooperative tense (T)/relaxed (R) framework applies, which outcome in the remaining occupied subunits would best be expected as a direct consequence of this single dissociation event?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Their conformation is unaffected, because each subunit binds and releases substrate independently of the others"}, {"label": "B", "text": "They shift toward the high-affinity relaxed (R) state, tightening their grip on the substrate still bound"}, {"label": "C", "text": "They shift toward the low-affinity tense (T) state, encouraging further substrate release"}, {"label": "D", "text": "They become irreversibly denatured, permanently abolishing all substrate binding"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because cooperativity is a linked, reversible equilibrium, so the same coupling that makes binding self-reinforcing also makes dissociation self-reinforcing in the opposite direction: losing a bound substrate shifts the remaining occupied subunits toward the low-affinity tense (T) state and promotes further release. LibreTexts assigns affinity to conformation explicitly, with the T form being low-affinity ("the binding of a ligand to the T form of a macromolecule, with KD = 100 uM (low affinity)") and the R form high-affinity ("the R form of the macromolecule with KD = 10 uM"), the two "linked through the T↔ R equilibrium" (lines 20800-20803). It states that "Without dioxygen, the T state (T0) is greatly favored over the unliganded R state (R0) at equilibrium. In the presence of increasing oxygen, the R state is favored" (lines 20663-20664); reading this equilibrium in reverse, removing bound ligand favors T. Because "the binding of one oxygen molecule increases the affinity of the remaining sites" (lines 20980-20982), the loss of a bound molecule must conversely lower the affinity of the remaining sites, the structural basis of which is that "the deoxy or T form is stabilized by eight salt bridges, broken in the transition to the oxy or R state" (line 20452) and reformed as the protein relaxes back toward T. (Choice A) is incorrect: independent, non-interacting sites describe non-cooperative, hyperbolic behavior, but the stem specifies positive cooperativity, in which a binding or release event at one subunit is communicated to the others through the T↔R equilibrium rather than leaving them unaffected (lines 20800-20803, 20980-20982). (Choice B) is incorrect: shifting toward the high-affinity R state and tightening substrate binding is precisely the response to substrate gain, not loss; binding of a molecule "increases the affinity of the remaining sites" (lines 20980-20982), so the dissociation described here drives the opposite, T-favoring direction (lines 20663-20664). (Choice C) is correct: as the equilibrium is pushed back toward the deoxy-like T conformation that is "greatly favored" without ligand (lines 20663-20664), the remaining occupied subunits adopt the low-affinity T state (KD = 100 uM, line 20800-20802) stabilized by reformed salt bridges (line 20452), which lowers their affinity and encourages additional substrate release. (Choice D) is incorrect: the T↔R interconversion is a reversible conformational equilibrium between two folded states (lines 20663-20664, 20800-20803), not an unfolding event; a single dissociation does not denature the enzyme or permanently abolish binding. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the reversible T↔R, low-versus-high-affinity model of cooperativity in the reverse direction, inferring how the loss of one bound substrate propagates a T-state, lower-affinity shift to the remaining occupied subunits.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
