-- Q17 [Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity', 'A researcher measures the catalytic rate of a purified enzyme across a range of assay temperatures and finds that activity rises from 0 to roughly 45 °C and then falls off sharply as the temperature climbs further. The single temperature at which this enzyme shows its greatest catalytic activity is best described by which term?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The enzyme''s optimal temperature"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The enzyme''s denaturation temperature"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The enzyme''s melting temperature"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The enzyme''s activation temperature"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because each enzyme has a single temperature at which its catalytic activity is greatest, which is termed its optimal temperature. LibreTexts states that ''from 0 to about 40-50 oC, enzyme activity usually increases, as do the rates of most reactions,'' and that ''at higher temperatures, the activity decreases dramatically as the enzyme denatures,'' a relationship presented in the figure titled ''Temperature effects on enzyme activity.'' Because activity rises to a peak near 45 °C and then falls, the peak of this curve is the temperature of maximal activity, which the text frames with ''optimal'' activity language for enzymes. (Choice A) is correct: the temperature giving maximal catalytic activity is the enzyme''s optimal temperature, the apex of the rise-then-fall activity-versus-temperature curve. (Choice B) is incorrect: the denaturation temperature is where heat unfolds the enzyme and ''the activity decreases dramatically as the enzyme denatures,'' so it marks the loss of activity, not its maximum. (Choice C) is incorrect: the melting temperature (Tm) is a protein-stability term defined as the temperature ''at which 50% of the proteins in the population are unfolded,'' describing the unfolding transition rather than the point of peak catalytic activity. (Choice D) is incorrect: ''activation temperature'' is not a defined kinetic term; the related concept is the activation energy that a catalyst lowers, so naming a temperature this way conflates activation energy with temperature and does not denote the activity maximum. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the temperature producing an enzyme''s maximal catalytic activity is termed its optimal temperature.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'opposite-of-maximal-activity' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'stability-term-not-activity-term' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', 'fabricated-term-from-real-concept' FROM q;

-- Q18 [Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity] medium skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity', 'A researcher measures the activity of a purified human enzyme as the assay temperature is raised. Activity climbs from 0 to roughly 40–50 °C, but as the temperature is pushed well above this optimum, the enzyme begins to unfold. Which of the following would the researcher most likely observe at these elevated temperatures?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Activity continues to rise indefinitely with temperature"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Activity falls off sharply as the enzyme denatures"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Activity remains essentially unchanged"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Activity is maintained while \\(K_m\\) decreases"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because raising the temperature well above an enzyme''s optimum unfolds (denatures) the protein, destroying the native active-site geometry and causing catalytic activity to drop steeply. LibreTexts states that "From 0 to about 40-50 oC, enzyme activity usually increases ... At higher temperatures, the activity decreases dramatically as the enzyme denatures" (lines 28491-28493), and the chapter summary adds that "Moderate increases in temperature typically enhance reaction rates, whereas excessive heat leads to irreversible denaturation" (lines 28568-28569). Thus, once temperature climbs past the optimum, the rising portion of the curve reverses and activity collapses as structure is lost. (Choice A) is wrong because activity does not keep rising indefinitely; the text explicitly says that above ~40-50 °C "the activity decreases dramatically as the enzyme denatures," so the curve turns downward rather than continuing upward. (Choice B) is correct: well above the optimum the enzyme denatures and "the activity decreases dramatically," producing the sharp fall-off the researcher observes. (Choice C) is wrong because activity is far from unchanged at these temperatures; denaturation "decreases" activity "dramatically," so a flat response contradicts the source. (Choice D) is wrong because excessive heat does not preserve activity with a tidy drop in \(K_m\); it causes "irreversible denaturation" and a dramatic loss of activity, and the text attributes the high-temperature decline to unfolding of the enzyme, not to a favorable change in substrate affinity. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that heating an enzyme above its optimum temperature denatures the protein and causes activity to fall sharply.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 85)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', NULL FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', NULL FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'scale_unit_error', NULL FROM q;

-- Q19 [Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity', 'A researcher assays a purified enzyme at a series of increasing temperatures, starting near 0 °C and continuing well past the enzyme''s physiological range, and plots the measured reaction velocity against temperature. Which of the following best describes the expected shape of this enzyme-activity-versus-temperature curve?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Activity increases continuously as temperature rises across the entire range tested"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Activity declines continuously from its highest value at the lowest temperature tested"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Activity rises to a maximum at an optimum temperature and then declines as temperature increases further"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Activity remains essentially constant regardless of the temperature"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because enzyme activity versus temperature is a peaked curve: the source states that ''From 0 to about 40-50 oC, enzyme activity usually increases, as do the rates of most reactions in the absence of catalysts,'' but that ''At higher temperatures, the activity decreases dramatically as the enzyme denatures'' (line 28491-28493). The same chapter summary reiterates that ''Moderate increases in temperature typically enhance reaction rates, whereas excessive heat leads to irreversible denaturation'' (line 28568-28569). Thus activity climbs to a maximum at an optimum temperature and then falls off as denaturation sets in. (Choice A) is wrong because activity does not increase continuously; once denaturation begins above the optimum, ''the activity decreases dramatically as the enzyme denatures'' (line 28492-28493), so the curve turns downward rather than rising across the whole range. (Choice B) is wrong because activity does not start at its peak and only decline; from 0 to roughly 40-50 °C ''enzyme activity usually increases'' (line 28491), so the low-temperature end is the rising portion, not the maximum. (Choice C) is correct: activity ''usually increases'' up to roughly 40-50 °C and then ''decreases dramatically as the enzyme denatures'' at higher temperatures (line 28491-28493), producing a curve that rises to an optimum and then declines. (Choice D) is wrong because activity is strongly temperature-dependent: it both increases with moderate warming and then drops with excessive heat (line 28491-28493, 28568-28569), so it is not constant. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how temperature affects enzyme activity, namely that activity rises to an optimum and then falls as heat denatures the enzyme.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 45)
  RETURNING id
)
