-- Q20 [Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity', 'A biochemist measures the initial velocity of a purified enzyme across a range of buffer pH values while holding temperature, ionic strength, and substrate concentration constant, and observes that activity rises to a single peak and then declines. Which of the following statements most accurately describes the general relationship between pH and enzyme activity that this result reflects?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Every enzyme exhibits maximal activity at pH 7, and any deviation from neutrality lowers its rate"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Changes in pH have no measurable effect on the velocity of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Enzyme activity rises continuously as pH increases, with no point of decline"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Each enzyme has a characteristic optimal pH at which activity is maximal, with deviations in either direction reducing activity"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because each enzyme has a characteristic optimal pH at which catalytic activity is greatest, and moving the pH above or below that optimum reduces activity. The textbook states that ''pH has a marked effect on the velocity of enzyme-catalyzed reactions'' (line 28499) and that a side chain ''must be deprotonated for optimal activity of trypsin, which shows increases in activity with pH centered at pH 6'' (line 28512). When activity is plotted against pH, ''the resulting curve... is bell-shaped, with the initial rise in activity reaching half-maximum activity at a pH of around 6.0 and a fall in activity reaching half-maximum at a pH of approximately 9'' (lines 30579-30580), so a single peak (optimum) is flanked by declining activity on both sides. Because the optimum reflects the protonation state of specific ionizable groups, it differs among enzymes rather than always being neutral. (Choice A) is wrong because the optimal pH is enzyme-specific, not universally pH 7: trypsin activity is ''centered at pH 6'' (line 28512), Glutathione-S-Transferase functions at an active-site pH of 7.5 (line 4871), and a chloroplast-stroma enzyme operates at pH 8.0 (line 4896), so deviations from neutrality do not uniformly lower activity. (Choice B) is wrong because pH demonstrably affects activity; the text notes that ''pH changes can alter the protonation state of key catalytic residues, thereby affecting both substrate binding and catalytic turnover'' (line 28570). (Choice C) is wrong because the activity-versus-pH relationship is ''bell-shaped'' with both a ''rise'' and a ''fall in activity'' (lines 30579-30580), not a monotonic increase. (Choice D) is correct: each enzyme has an optimal pH at which activity is maximal, and the bell-shaped profile shows that ''a deprotonated histidine is necessary for optimal catalysis'' (lines 30634-30635) while deviations to either side reduce the rate. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that enzyme activity depends on pH through the protonation states of catalytic residues, producing a characteristic optimal pH for each enzyme.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
