-- Q21 [Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity', 'A researcher measuring the activity of a purified enzyme finds that its rate is maximal near pH 7 but drops sharply when the assay buffer is shifted to pH 2 or pH 12, with little recovery after the protein is returned to pH 7. Which of the following best explains why catalytic activity falls so steeply at a pH far from the enzyme''s optimum?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Extreme pH alters the ionization (protonation state) of key active-site residues and can denature the enzyme."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Extreme pH changes the molecular formula of the substrate so the enzyme can no longer recognize it."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Extreme pH forms new covalent bonds within the enzyme that lock it into an active conformation."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Extreme pH increases the enzyme''s \\(V_{max}\\) so the measured rate overshoots and reads as a loss of activity."}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because LibreTexts explains that pH controls enzyme activity by setting the charge of catalytic side chains and, at extremes, by unfolding the protein. The chapter''s learning goals state the aim to ''Describe how temperature and pH changes affect enzyme activity, including the role of pH in altering the protonation states of key catalytic residues'' (lines 27778-27779), and the summary reiterates that ''pH changes can alter the protonation state of key catalytic residues, thereby affecting both substrate binding and catalytic turnover'' (line 28570). The text further notes that ''The easiest assumption is that key side chains necessary for catalysis must be in the correct protonation state,'' citing trypsin, whose activity is centered around pH 6 because a side chain of apparent pKa ~6 ''must be deprotonated for optimal activity'' (lines 28511-28512). At the far extremes, the enzyme is also denatured: ''extremes of pH and high temperature, all of which can denature the enzyme, would irreversibly inhibit the enzyme unless it could refold properly'' (lines 27795-27796), which matches the poor recovery seen in the stem. (Choice A) is correct: away from the optimum, the active-site residues are forced into the wrong ionization state and, at extremes, the protein denatures, so catalysis collapses. (Choice B) is incorrect: pH changes the protonation state, not the molecular formula, of the substrate—the text lists that pH can ''affect S by altering the protonation state of the substrate'' (line 28510), which is a reversible charge change, not a permanent change in chemical formula or identity. (Choice C) is incorrect: pH-driven loss of activity is structural denaturation, not the creation of new covalent bonds; LibreTexts treats covalent modification of side chains (e.g., iodoacetamide reacting with a cysteine, lines 27797-27800) as a separate, distinct mechanism of inhibition from pH/temperature denaturation, and no new covalent bonds are formed simply by changing pH. (Choice D) is incorrect: activity falls rather than rises at extreme pH, so \(V_{max}\) is not increased—the text states that with temperature ''the activity decreases dramatically as the enzyme denatures'' (lines 28492-28493) and that pH ''has a marked effect on the velocity of enzyme-catalyzed reactions'' (line 28499), consistent with the measured drop, not an overshoot. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the textbook''s account of how pH sets the ionization state of catalytic residues and how extreme pH denatures the enzyme to explain the observed steep, poorly reversible loss of activity far from the optimum.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
