-- Q24 [Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity] medium skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity', 'An enzymologist transfers a purified, fully folded enzyme from a physiological buffer into a solution containing a high concentration of a chaotropic salt and observes that the enzyme rapidly loses catalytic activity. Which of the following best describes how the high salt concentration most likely affects the enzyme?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It forms new covalent bonds within the enzyme that lock it into an inactive shape"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It lowers the activation energy of the reaction the enzyme catalyzes"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It has essentially no effect on the enzyme''s folded structure"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It disrupts the ionic and hydrogen-bonding interactions that stabilize the folded conformation, altering the enzyme''s structure"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because a high concentration of a chaotropic salt disrupts the weak noncovalent interactions that hold a protein in its native, active fold. LibreTexts lists the agents that unfold or denature proteins as ''pH extremes, high concentrations of some salt solutions or organic solvents, and temperature extremes'' (line 14703), and explains that ion pairs in proteins are salt-sensitive: ''If ion pairs are the source of protein stability, you would expect that high salt could disrupt them and lead to denaturation'' (lines 14766-14767). The text further states that high concentrations of chaotropic salts ''disrupt water structure and hydrogen bonding'' and that ''The salts also denature proteins'' (line 41124). Because a salt bridge is defined as an ''ion-ion interaction'' that is categorized as a ''noncovalent attraction'' (lines 3938-3939), disrupting these ionic and hydrogen-bonding interactions changes the enzyme''s conformation and abolishes activity. (Choice A) is incorrect: salt does not create covalent bonds; the interactions stabilizing the fold are noncovalent (''ion-ion noncovalent attraction,'' lines 3938-3939), and denaturation by salt is distinct from ''irreversible covalent modification'' (line 27747). (Choice B) is incorrect: lowering activation energy is what an enzyme does as a catalyst (''how they lower activation energies and speed'' reactions, line 659), not an effect that added salt has on the enzyme itself. (Choice C) is incorrect: this directly contradicts the text, which names ''high concentrations of some salt solutions'' among the agents that denature proteins (line 14703) and states that chaotropic salts ''denature proteins'' (line 41124). (Choice D) is correct: a high concentration of a chaotropic salt disrupts the ionic (salt-bridge) and hydrogen-bonding interactions that stabilize the native fold, altering the enzyme''s conformation and reducing activity (lines 14703, 14766-14767, 41124, 3938-3939). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that high salt concentrations denature enzymes by disrupting the noncovalent ionic and hydrogen-bonding interactions that stabilize protein conformation.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
